In this essay I will discuss the commodity in Marxian economics, and how it relates to
exploitation, the tendency of the rate of profit to fall and finally, alienation of the labourer
from their work.
In the first chapter of Das Kapital Vol I, Marx discusses the dualities of the commodity. The
first of these dualities is the duality of use-value and exchange-value. The use value is
defined as the “material depositories” of exchange value and is ultimately the utility of a
given commodity - what it can be used for. For example a coat can be worn and coffee
grains can be used to make coffee. The exchange value is the value of commodities
expressed in quantitative ratio to each other (Marx 1867, pp. 27). Commodities choose an
equivalent for themselves in the course of value relation via this very process of social
convention and out of this relationship the exchange value comes into existence (Marx
1867, pp. 34). Pilling (1980) points out that Marx is fundamentally trying to express the
idea that ultimately commodities are expressions, in a fetishistic form, of definite social
conditions. Exchange value is created by labour power because labour power is the only
commodity capable of creating more value than itself. This itself is also the reason labour
power becomes a commodity: because it is a source of value (Dunayevskaya 1979) This
is best expressed in the equation Marx uses: 20 yards of linen = 1 coat. In this equation
the 20 yards of linen is equal to one coat because both of them embody the same amount
of socially necessary labour time needed to create them. Commodities of equivalent
exchange value are commodities of equivalent labour time, barring in the case of
commodity fetishism in which the socially necessary labour does not account for market
value because the price of a commodity is confused with its value (Rubin 1928). In any
case, the equation illustrates both the social convention at play in exchange and how
exchange value determines the value of commodities. Derrida (1994) points out that while
Marx deals with the "phantomalisation" of the commodity-form in Das Kapital, he also
deals with the significance of social bonds with regards the commodity. Marx ultimately
believes that the commodity form results in the process whereby individuals relate to each
other through the process of exchange. As a result social interaction becomes about these
exchanges rather than genuine human connection, resulting in alienation from each other
and the products of our labour

The second duality of the commodity is the duality between concrete labour and abstract
labour. These two are also closely linked to “private” and “social” labour where private
labour is the labour done by individuals that expresses itself as universal abstract labour in
