the creation of exchange value, it thereby becomes social labour (Marx 1867, pp. 209).
Rubin (1928) defines social labour as the "substance" expressed in the value of a
commodity because in a commodity economy value is a social relation between people.
According to Rubin, abstract labour is the labour that creates value and it expresses the
relationship between social labour and the creation of value. It is considered abstract
because all labour is reduced to the common denominator of the socially necessary labour
time expressed in commodities, it is only through this abstraction that labour can create
any value (Dunayevskaya 1979). The concrete labour is therefore the specific labour that
is required to create the use-value of commodities. Private labour is thus transformed from
it's concrete origins into universal abstract labour because the commodities created at the
private concrete level have a social value once they can be expressed in the ratio of
exchange values. It's important to note that Marx does not think the difference between
skilled and unskilled labour is significant within his theories. This is because neither have
the homogeneity that abstract labour does and are not directly related to exchange value
(Kidron 1968).

This all relates itself very closely to exploitation. The labour theory of value states that
value is determined by the amount of socially necessary labour time needed to make the
commodity in a given spector assuming average skill and organic composition of capital
(Mandel 1990). This is expressed in the following equations, first the rate of surplus value,
which Marx terms the “real degree of exploitation of labour” (Marx 1867, pp. 373): S/v
where s= surplus value and v= variable capital (wages paid). Here surplus value is the
representation of the value that the capitalist collects from the work that the labourer does,
because a small part of the value created by labour power must be paid to the worker in
order for them to sustain themselves. Secondly, there is the equation of the rate of profit
which is expressed as s/(c+v) where s=surplus value, c=constant capital (means of
production) and v=variable capital (wages paid) (Marx 1867, pp. 150) . Both of these
equations tell us that the rate of profit and thereby surplus value are contingent upon the
exploitation of labour power; the capitalist cannot profit if they do not exploit the surplus
labour of the worker. Thus by being the only commodity capable of creating any value, the
labour power of the worker is the very foundation of capitalism and its ability to create
more profit. Marx illustrates this perfectly in the 18th chapter of Das Kapital. In it, while
discussing formulas that deal with surplus-value, Marx provides an example wherein a
English agricultural labourer only gets ¼ of the value they created whereas the capitalist
