gets ¾ of the value. Marx, using derivative formulae of surplus value, calculates the rate of
exploitation in this example to be 300% (Marx 1867, pp. 373-374)

In addition, this is all related to Marx’s concept of the tendency of the rate of profit to fall.
The tendency of the rate of profit to fall is the result of a conflict between the organic
composition of capital and the creation of surplus value (Marx 1894, pp. 154). The organic
composition of capital is the ratio of constant capital to variable capital and expresses itself
as the output of production not related to labour power itself, it is “capital intensive
production” and cannot create its own surplus value because it is not labour power. As
Capitalists invest more in constant capital relative to variable capital the rate of profit
declines because only variable capital can actually produce any value. Therefore, as
automation displaces more and more labour, the amount of profit (surplus value) created
will decrease. As a result of this tendency of the rate of profit to fall inherent in the increase
in the organic composition of capital, the capitalist is forced to further the exploitation of the
worker’s labour power commodity in order to make up for the profit lost in the process of
investing in constant capital by making them work more surplus labour time. As the
amount of necessary labour time decreases (due to automation) the amount of surplus
labour time must necessarily increase to offset the fall in surplus value created by the
automation, hence the tendency of the rate of profit to fall is an inherent factor in the
exploitation of the worker. Jim Miller (1995) points out that Marx defines this tendency as a
"necessary consequence of the evolutionary logic of the capitalist mode of production". In
this sense, this model of capitalist exploitation is inherently built into the system and
commodity form itself. The point: capitalism is a commodity economy, commodities only
get value from surplus labour, surplus labour is inherently the exploitation of the workers
surplus value, and therefore the commodity form in capitalism necessitates the exploitation
of the worker.

Because the worker is forced to work long hours in order to create surplus value, they feel
alienated from their work. This is the result of not owning their own means of production
and being underpaid relative to the value they create. In “Marx’s Concept of Man” (1961)
Erich Fromm defines Marx’s concept of alienation as the worker who does not experience
themselves as an agent acting within their grasp of the world but the world itself feels alien
to the worker. Alienation is closely related to exploitation, as it is the exploitation itself that
can drive the gap between the worker and the world, and make it feel alien to them. Balci
